  "question": {
    "stem": "A 59-year-old man who self-identifies as Black is evaluated for exertional dyspnea and fatigue despite 4 months of treatment for nonischemic HFrEF. He must stop after walking two blocks but has no symptoms at rest. Medications are maximally tolerated sacubitril-valsartan, carvedilol, dapagliflozin, and furosemide. He has had no HF hospitalization during the past year.\n\nTemperature 36.8 C, BP 118/72 mm Hg, HR 66/min, RR 16/min, and SpO2 97% on room air. JVP is 7 cm H2O. Lungs are clear, and there is no peripheral edema.\n\nECG shows sinus rhythm. Echocardiography shows an LVEF of 28% without significant valvular disease. Sodium is 138 mEq/L, potassium 5.4 mEq/L, creatinine 2.6 mg/dL, and eGFR 27 mL/min/1.73 m². Potassium has remained above 5.0 mEq/L on repeated testing.\n\nWhich of the following is the most appropriate medication to add?",
    "choices": [
      {
        "letter": "A",
        "text": "Digoxin",
        "correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "B",
        "text": "Fixed-dose hydralazine-isosorbide dinitrate",
        "correct": true
      },
      {
        "letter": "C",
        "text": "Ivabradine",
        "correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "D",
        "text": "Spironolactone",
        "correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "E",
        "text": "Vericiguat",
        "correct": false
      }
    ],
    "correct_letter": "B",
    "explanation": "The 2022 AHA/ACC/HFSA guideline states that fixed-dose hydralazine-isosorbide dinitrate is recommended to improve symptoms and reduce morbidity and mortality in self-identified Black patients with NYHA class III-IV HFrEF receiving optimal medical therapy (Class 1, level A). [1]\n\nThis patient has persistent NYHA class III symptoms despite maximally tolerated ARNI, evidence-based BB, and SGLT2 inhibitor therapy. An MRA would ordinarily complete the four foundational HFrEF therapies, but guideline-recommended initiation requires eGFR greater than 30 mL/min/1.73 m² and potassium less than 5.0 mEq/L. His eGFR is 27 and potassium is persistently 5.4 mEq/L, making spironolactone unsafe. [1]\n\nA-HeFT supports adding fixed-dose hydralazine-isosorbide dinitrate in this clinical population. [2] Ivabradine requires sinus rhythm with a resting HR of at least 70/min despite a maximally tolerated BB. Vericiguat is a weaker, Class 2b option considered after recent worsening HF, which is absent here. Digoxin may reduce hospitalizations in persistently symptomatic HFrEF but has no established mortality benefit and is less appropriate than the specifically indicated Class 1 therapy.",
    "wrong_explanations": [
      {
        "letter": "A",
        "why": "Digoxin may reduce HF hospitalizations but does not improve survival. It is a lower-strength option and should not displace hydralazine-isosorbide dinitrate in a patient meeting the latter therapy's Class 1 indication."
      },
      {
        "letter": "C",
        "why": "Ivabradine may be considered for symptomatic HFrEF with LVEF 35% or less, sinus rhythm, and resting HR at least 70/min despite a maximally tolerated BB. This patient's HR is 66/min."
      },
      {
        "letter": "D",
        "why": "MRA initiation is recommended only when eGFR is greater than 30 mL/min/1.73 m² and potassium is less than 5.0 mEq/L. This patient's persistent hyperkalemia and reduced eGFR create excessive risk."
      },
      {
        "letter": "E",
        "why": "Vericiguat is a Class 2b option for selected high-risk patients with recent worsening HF despite GDMT. This patient has had no recent hospitalization or decompensation and has a stronger therapy-specific indication."
      }
    ],
    "difficulty_level": 4,
    "difficulty_label": "Board-level",
    "difficulty_rationale": "The solver must identify persistent advanced symptoms, recognize the contraindication to a missing foundational therapy, and distinguish a Class 1 race- and symptom-specific treatment from several plausible adjuncts.",
    "reasoning_steps": [
      "Identify persistent NYHA class III HFrEF despite maximally tolerated ARNI, evidence-based BB, and SGLT2 inhibitor therapy.",
      "Recognize that persistent potassium elevation and eGFR below 30 preclude safe initiation of an MRA.",
      "Use the self-identified Black race and NYHA class III symptoms to identify the guideline-specific indication for fixed-dose hydralazine-isosorbide dinitrate.",
      "Exclude ivabradine because HR is below 70/min and exclude vericiguat because there has been no recent worsening HF event."
    ]
  }
}

{
  "title": "Occult GI Evaluation in Iron Deficiency Anemia",
  "subtitle": "Why negative stool testing does not end the evaluation",
  "guideline_sources": [
    "AGA Clinical Practice Guidelines on the Gastrointestinal Evaluation of Iron Deficiency Anemia, 2020"
  ],
  "key_point": "Men and postmenopausal women with iron deficiency anemia should undergo bidirectional endoscopy even without GI symptoms or overt bleeding. [1]",
  "abim_classification": {
    "category": "Hematology",
    "subcategory": "Anemia",
    "specific_topic": "Iron deficiency anemia: GI evaluation",
    "blueprint_weight": "6%"
  },
  "board_pearls": [
    "Chronic occult GI blood loss depletes iron stores before producing overt bleeding or GI symptoms.",
    "The 2020 AGA guideline strongly recommends bidirectional endoscopy in asymptomatic men with iron deficiency anemia, based on moderate-certainty evidence. [1]",
    "Colonoscopy alone is inadequate because peptic ulcer disease and upper GI malignancy remain possible, particularly with NSAID exposure.",
    "The AGA recommends a ferritin cutoff below 45 ng/mL when diagnosing iron deficiency in patients with anemia; this patient's ferritin is 8 ng/mL. [1]",
    "A negative fecal immunochemical test does not exclude the GI lesions responsible for iron deficiency anemia and should not delay endoscopy."
  ],
  "teaching_points": [
    "Iron deficiency anemia is established by microcytosis, increased RDW, low ferritin, low serum iron, and low transferrin saturation.",
    "Guideline-recommended evaluation in men and postmenopausal women examines both the upper and lower GI tracts, preferably during the same session. [1]",
    "Iron replacement may begin during the evaluation, but treatment alone is insufficient because it does not identify a potential bleeding lesion or malignancy."
  ],
  "summary_points": [
    "Confirmed iron deficiency anemia in an older man warrants EGD and colonoscopy regardless of absent GI symptoms, NSAID use, or negative stool blood testing. [1]"
  ],
  "visual_memory_card": {
    "top_left": "Older man with confirmed iron deficiency",
    "top_right": "Occult GI loss depletes iron stores",
    "bottom_left": "Evaluate both upper and lower tracts",
    "bottom_right": "Negative FIT does not exclude bleeding",
    "center": ""
  },
  "references": [
    {
      "id": 1,
      "source": "AGA Clinical Practice Guidelines on the Gastrointestinal Evaluation of Iron Deficiency Anemia",
      "year": 2020,
      "society": "American Gastroenterological Association",
      "url": "https://doi.org/10.1053/j.gastro.2020.06.046",
      "type": "guideline"
    }
  ],
  "question": {
    "stem": "A 67-year-old man is evaluated in the office for 3 months of progressive fatigue and reduced exercise tolerance. He has no abdominal pain, dysphagia, change in bowel habits, melena, or hematochezia. He takes naproxen several times weekly for knee osteoarthritis and no anticoagulant. A screening fecal immunochemical test 2 weeks ago was negative; his last colonoscopy 12 years ago was normal.\n\nTemperature 36.8 C, BP 128/74 mm Hg, HR 82/min, RR 14/min, and SpO2 99% on room air. He has conjunctival pallor. Abdominal and rectal examinations are normal.\n\nHemoglobin is 10.2 g/dL, MCV 72 fL, RDW 18%, ferritin 8 ng/mL, serum iron 24 µg/dL, and total iron-binding capacity 420 µg/dL. Creatinine and liver tests are normal.\n\nWhich of the following is the most appropriate next step in management?",
    "choices": [
      {
        "letter": "A",
        "text": "Bone marrow biopsy",
        "correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "B",
        "text": "Capsule endoscopy",
        "correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "C",
        "text": "Colonoscopy alone",
        "correct": false
      },
      {
        "letter": "D",
        "text": "EGD and colonoscopy",
        "correct": true
      },
      {
        "letter": "E",
        "text": "Oral iron therapy alone",
        "correct": false
      }
    ],
    "correct_letter": "D",
    "explanation": "The 2020 AGA guideline strongly recommends bidirectional endoscopy—EGD and colonoscopy—over no endoscopy in asymptomatic men with iron deficiency anemia, based on moderate-certainty evidence. It also recommends using a ferritin cutoff below 45 ng/mL to diagnose iron deficiency in patients with anemia. [1]\n\nThis patient has unequivocal iron deficiency anemia: low hemoglobin, microcytosis, increased RDW, ferritin of 8 ng/mL, low serum iron, and increased iron-binding capacity. In an older man, chronic occult GI blood loss must be investigated for upper and lower GI causes, including peptic ulcer disease, angioectasia, polyps, and malignancy. His naproxen exposure increases the possibility of an upper GI lesion but does not eliminate the need for colonoscopy. Conversely, a negative fecal immunochemical test does not reliably exclude a bleeding lesion in a patient with established iron deficiency anemia.\n\nIron replacement can be started, but it must not substitute for the indicated GI evaluation. Small-bowel testing is generally reserved for persistent or recurrent iron deficiency after an unrevealing initial evaluation.",
    "wrong_explanations": [
      {
        "letter": "A",
        "why": "Bone marrow biopsy is unnecessary because peripheral blood indices and iron studies already establish iron deficiency. It is reserved for unexplained anemia or suspected marrow disease."
      },
      {
        "letter": "B",
        "why": "Capsule endoscopy evaluates the small bowel but is not the initial study. It is considered after unrevealing bidirectional endoscopy when iron deficiency persists or recurs."
      },
      {
        "letter": "C",
        "why": "Colonoscopy evaluates colorectal disease but misses upper GI causes such as NSAID-related ulceration and gastric malignancy. Men with iron deficiency anemia require bidirectional evaluation."
      },
      {
        "letter": "E",
        "why": "Oral iron is appropriate treatment but cannot be the sole intervention in an older man because correction of anemia would not exclude an occult GI malignancy or bleeding lesion."
      }
    ],
    "difficulty_level": 4,
    "difficulty_label": "Board-level",
    "difficulty_rationale": "The solver must confirm true iron deficiency, recognize the need to investigate occult blood loss, disregard a falsely reassuring negative stool test, and select bidirectional rather than lower-only endoscopy.",
    "reasoning_steps": [
      "Identify iron deficiency anemia from microcytosis, increased RDW, low ferritin, low serum iron, and increased iron-binding capacity.",
      "Recognize that iron deficiency anemia in an older man requires evaluation for occult GI blood loss even without GI symptoms.",
      "Determine that a negative fecal immunochemical test does not replace diagnostic endoscopy in established iron deficiency anemia.",
      "Apply the AGA recommendation for bidirectional endoscopy rather than colonoscopy alone, small-bowel testing, or iron replacement alone."
    ]
  }
}
